Hello,
This is not a new thread; but like others before i tried all i could think of.
I am trying poppr to import a .csv genalex file (read.genalex). The file has 60 populations, 6 regions and geographical coordinates. I can import the dataset by setting region=F but not region=T, and it becomes impossible to set region a posteriori with splitStrata etc.

The error message that i receive when using region=T is:

acropora1 <- read.genalex("/Users/joana/Documents/CLASSES/2ndSemester_2018-2019/Datasets/Corail_Australie_msats/atenuis_good_genalex.csv",
+                           ploidy=2, region=T, sep=",", geo = T)
Error in data.frame(Pop = pop.vec, Region = reg.vec) :
  arguments imply differing number of rows: 1936, 0

Is this basically saying it cannot find the regions data? I wonder why is that, since the separator is the underscore.

And the error when setting region=F and then using splitStrata:

splitStrata(acropora1) <- ~pop/region
Error:
  Data has 1 level of strata with the separator _ .
  Here is the fist column of the data: 01_north

I have used several other files formatted in the very same way (that i can see) with no errors (i'm preparing datasets for classes).
Thanks very much in advance  for any help.
Kind regards,
Joana Boavida

On Wednesday, 6 February 2019 04:57:42 UTC+1, Zhian Kamvar wrote:
> Hi Carlolina,
>
> In the second row, starting with the fourth column (B4–B7 in excel) you need to put the names of the populations each size represents as shown in https://grunwaldlab.github.io/Population_Genetics_in_R/Data_Preparation.html#basic-format
>
> Genalex files have a lot of redundancy, so poppr will use that information if it's available. In your case, you specified the sizes of the populations (A4–A7), but not which populations they belong to (B4–B7)
>
> Hope that helps!
> Zhian
